{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Mild positive bias at short horizons (≈D+1 to D+10): model tends to run ~0.2–1.0 EUR above realized levels; apply a small downward shift early in the path.",
    "Systematic underreaction to upside breakouts at longer horizons (≈D+20 to D+30): when the market trends up, the model mean-reverts and ends up ~3–9 EUR too low by the last week; add an upward trend/level adjustment that grows with horizon during strong uptrends.",
    "Turning-point lag: model often anticipates a softening/decline that occurs later or not at all; delay any forecasted downturn by ~3–7 days unless confirmed by recent realized weakness.",
    "Overshoot on local peaks, then excessive mean reversion: around short-term highs the model is slightly too high, but then pulls back too aggressively; dampen peak-day overshoots and reduce subsequent reversion speed.",
    "Regime rule: if the last 5–10 realized days show a persistent higher-high/higher-low pattern, reduce mean-reversion strength and increase drift; if choppy/flat, keep mean reversion and apply the small early-horizon downward bias."
  ]
}
